69: We have examined star formation in two very luminous ($M_V=-22$ to $-23$)
70: Sc-type spiral galaxies, NGC 801 and UGC 2885, using ultra-deep \ha\ images.
71: We combine these with $UBV$ and 2MASS $JHK$ images and \HI\ maps to explore the star formation characteristics
72: of disk galaxies at high luminosity.
73: \ha\ traces star formation in these galaxies to 4-6 disk scale lengths,
74: but the lack of detection of \ha\ further out is likely due to loss of Lyman continuum photons.
75: Considering gravitational instabilities alone, we find that the gas and stars in the outer regions are marginally
76: stable in an average sense, but considering dissipative gas and radial and azimuthal forcing, the outer regions are 
77: marginally unstable to form spiral arms. 
78: Star formation is taking place in spiral arms, which are regions of locally higher gas densities.
79: Furthermore, we have traced smooth exponential stellar disks over 3-orders of magnitude and 4-6 disk scale lengths,
80: in spite of a highly variable gravitational instability parameter. Thus, gravitational instability thresholds
81: do not seem relevant to the stellar disk.
82: One possibility for creating an exponential disk is that
83: the molecular cloud densities and star formation rates have exponential profiles and this forces the stellar disk to build up such a profile.

